                <p class="align-left">for <span style="color: #b96ad9;">Batocera</span> and <span style="color: #169179;">Retrobat</span></p>

<figure class="post__image post__image--center"><img decoding="auto" loading="lazy"  src="https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/346/segachannel_logo.png" alt="" width="300" height="275" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" srcset="https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/346/responsive/segachannel_logo-xs.webp 300w ,https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/346/responsive/segachannel_logo-sm.webp 480w ,https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/346/responsive/segachannel_logo-md.webp 768w ,https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/346/responsive/segachannel_logo-xl.webp 1200w"></figure>
<p> </p>
<figure class="post__image post__image--right"><img decoding="auto" loading="lazy"  style="margin-bottom: 1.37143rem; margin-left: 1.37143rem; outline: 3px solid rgba(var(--color-primary-rgb), 0.55) !important;" src="https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/346/sega-channel-up.webp" alt="" width="1256" height="1256" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" srcset="https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/346/responsive/sega-channel-up-xs.webp 300w ,https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/346/responsive/sega-channel-up-sm.webp 480w ,https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/346/responsive/sega-channel-up-md.webp 768w ,https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/346/responsive/sega-channel-up-xl.webp 1200w"></figure>
<p>Sega Channel was an online game service developed by Sega for the Sega Genesis video game console, functioning as a content delivery system. Launched on December 12, 1994, Sega Channel was provided to users by TCI and Time Warner Cable through cable television services via coaxial cable. It was a paid service that allowed users to access Genesis games online, play demos, and use cheat codes.<br><br>The service operated until July 31, 1998—continuing for three years after the release of Sega’s next-generation console, the Sega Saturn. Despite criticism for its poorly timed launch and high subscription cost, Sega Channel received recognition for its innovations in downloadable content and its influence on the development of online gaming services.<br><br>Of particular interest to enthusiasts is a collection that also includes dozens of previously unreleased game variants and Sega Channel exclusives. This compilation features Garfield: Caught in the Act – The Lost Levels and The Flintstones, two games that were previously considered permanently lost and unrecoverable.</p>

<figure class="post__video"><iframe loading="lazy" width="560" height="314" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/OcsRIoGeXr8" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen" data-mce-fragment="1"></iframe></figure>
<h5>The package includes:</h5>
<p class="msg msg--success">97 games (~2,23 GB)</p>
