<figure class="post__image post__image--center"><img decoding="auto"  src="https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/339/dice-w.svg" alt="" width="347" height="112"></figure><br><figure class="post__image post__image--right"><img decoding="auto" loading="lazy"  src="https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/339/dice.png" alt="" width="1080" height="1080" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" srcset="https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/339/responsive/dice-xs.webp 300w ,https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/339/responsive/dice-sm.webp 480w ,https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/339/responsive/dice-md.webp 768w ,https://pixelnostalgia.github.io/media/posts/339/responsive/dice-xl.webp 1200w"></figure>
<p>Discover the earliest arcade games ever made — now playable thanks to the new DICE emulator!</p>
<p>DICE (Discrete Integrated Circuit Emulator) emulates old arcade machines from the early 1970's at a very high level of accuracy. Since these machines had no CPU, the emulator instead emulates discrete logic components in the circuit board.</p>
<p>This method is very system-intensive, and getting full speed requires at least a mid-range CPU (for single-thread performance) along with the 64-bit version of the emulator.</p>

<h5>The package includes:</h5>
<p class="msg msg--success">21 games (~107 MB)</p>
